3.Explain arrays in bash.


Arrays in Bash are ordered collections of variables, typically one-dimensional, where elements are accessed using numeric indices starting from 0. Bash also supports associative arrays (using declare -A) which use strings as keys.
1D Example: names=("Alice" "Bob" "Charlie")

4.Display array elements.

You can loop through all elements of an array using the special @ index notation within a for loop.
my_array=(a b c d)
for element in "${my_array[@]}"; do
echo "$element"
done


5.Purpose of cron.

Cron is the utility used to schedule repetitive jobs or scripts (cron jobs) on a regular, predefined basis, such as hourly, daily, weekly, or at specific times.

6.Cron job to run backup daily at midnight.

0 0 * * * /home/user/backup.sh

(This cron entry runs the script at 0 minutes past the 0th hour, every day of the month, every month, and every day of the week.)

7. Schedule one-time job using at.

The at command is used to schedule a script or command to run once at a specific time in the future.
echo "/home/user/task.sh" | at 02:30

This schedules /home/user/task.sh to run today at 2:30 AM.

8.Script to display disk usage.

To display the total disk space usage of filesystems in a human-readable format:
df -h

To display the summarized size of all files and directories in the current path:
du -sh *


9.Log output using tee.


The tee command reads standard input and simultaneously writes it to standard output (the terminal) and to one or more files. It's often used to capture the output of a running script while viewing it in real-time.
./myscript.sh | tee logfile.log


